WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.777
Coding Exercise 3 asks you to build a searched CASE with clear categories and boundaries.

00:00:05.777 --> 00:00:08.082
Start with the exceptional states.

00:00:08.082 --> 00:00:11.072
If bill amount is NULL, return Unknown.

00:00:11.072 --> 00:00:14.817
If the amount is exactly zero, return No Charge.

00:00:14.817 --> 00:00:19.437
Then apply the high-value threshold, followed by the standard threshold.

00:00:19.437 --> 00:00:23.177
Use ELSE for the remaining positive low-value bills.

00:00:23.177 --> 00:00:27.337
The order matters because CASE stops at the first true branch.

00:00:27.337 --> 00:00:34.497
After you run the query, inspect records that exercise different branches: a missing bill, a zero bill,

00:00:34.497 --> 00:00:37.462
a high-value bill, and a standard bill.

00:00:37.462 --> 00:00:43.577
If a zero bill shows as Low-Value, the zero branch is missing or positioned incorrectly.

00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:48.777
If a missing bill becomes Low-Value, you did not handle NULL explicitly.

00:00:48.777 --> 00:00:55.252
This is a good exercise in building a small decision tree and then testing one representative record from

00:00:55.252 --> 00:00:55.760
each branch.
